UPDATE results SET statement_tex = 'Let $\chi$ and $\psi$ be \hyperref[8JH4]{primitive characters}, let $\mathcal C_\psi$, $a_\chi$ and $\mathcal G_\psi$ be as in the \hyperref[8JG8]{definition of the cyclic family}, and let $b_\chi = L''(1,\chi)/L(1,\chi)$ for $\chi \ne \mathbf 1$. The \hyperref[8JS2]{Mellin transforms}
\[ H_\chi = \mathcal M G_\chi, \qquad \mathcal H_\psi = \mathcal M \mathcal G_\psi \]
of the \hyperref[8JF7]{smoothed prime sum} and of the \hyperref[8JG8]{weighted sum} are \hyperref[8JS3]{defined for $\Re s > 1$}, and there:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \begin{align*}
H_\chi(s) &= -\frac{L''(s,\chi)}{L(s,\chi)}\frac{1}{s(s-1)} + \frac{b_\chi}{s-1} && (\chi \ne \mathbf 1), \\
H_{\mathbf 1}(s) &= -\frac{\zeta''(s)}{\zeta(s)}\frac{1}{s(s-1)} - \frac{1}{(s-1)^2} + \frac{1+\gamma}{s-1}.
\end{align*}
\item[(ii)] \[ \mathcal H_\psi(s) = \frac12 \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi \bigl(H_\chi(s) + H_{\bar\chi}(s)\bigr). \]
\end{enumerate}', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 119;

UPDATE results SET statement_tex = 'For each prime $p$ let $I_p\subseteq B$ be its inertia group and $F_p$ a representative of its Frobenius coset. For $t\in A$ and $g\notin\Phi$ set $p_t(g)=g+((1,1),t)\in\Phi$. For real $a>0$ and weights $w=(w_\alpha)_{\alpha\in\Phi}$ with $w_\alpha>0$, set
$$
S(t,a,w)=\sum_{p,\ i\ge 1}\sum_{g\notin\Phi}
\frac{2\mathbf 1_{g-iF_p\in I_p}}{|I_p|}\ln p\,
\left(\frac{a}{p^i}-w_{p_t(g)}\right)_+.
$$
Here $v_+=\max(v,0)$ for real $v$. Write $\xi_c$ for the sign character of the first coordinate, using its primitive representative in $L(s,\xi_c)$, and set
$$
M(a,w)=a\left(\ln a-1-\gamma+\frac{L''(1,\xi_c)}{L(1,\xi_c)}
-\frac1f\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi}\ln w_\alpha\right).
$$', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 128;

UPDATE results SET statement_tex = 'In the normalization of \cite[Section 8]{Outline}, write $A_* =\sum_t a_t$, $\ell=\Re(L''(1,\xi_c)/L(1,\xi_c))$, and let $v_0$ be the geometric normalization parameter. The divisibility main term is
$$
D_{\rm div}=m\sum_t a_t(\ln a_t-1-\gamma-\ln(\pi/v_0)+\ell).
$$
The threshold is $\lambda=\sum_j f^{-1}\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi}\ln(w_{j,\alpha}v_0/\pi)+mg_0-E_{\rm count}$, where $g_0$ is the common logarithmic index term and $E_{\rm count}$ the error in the counting lower bound. Then
$$
D_{\rm div}-A_*\lambda+mA_*g_0
=\sum_{t,j}\Re M(a_t,w_j)+A_*E_{\rm count}.
$$', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 131;

UPDATE results SET statement_tex = 'Take $\psi = \mathbf 1$, the \hyperref[8JH4]{trivial character}, so that $L(s, \mathbf 1) = \zeta(s)$ and $o = 1$. The \hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} is $\mathcal C_{\mathbf 1} = \{\mathbf 1\}$ with weight $a_{\mathbf 1} = 1$, so the weighted sum is the \hyperref[8JF7]{smoothed prime sum} of the \hyperref[8JS9]{trivial character}:
\[ \mathcal G_{\mathbf 1}(x) = G_{\mathbf 1}(x) = \sum_{n \le x} \Lambda(n)\Bigl(\frac xn - 1\Bigr) - x\ln x + (1+\gamma)x. \]

\emph{Poles.} By \ref{8JS6} and \ref{8JN4}, the Mellin transform
\[ H_{\mathbf 1}(s) = -\frac{\zeta''(s)}{\zeta(s)}\,\frac{1}{s(s-1)} - \frac{1}{(s-1)^2} + \frac{1+\gamma}{s-1} \]
has a simple pole at each nontrivial zero $\rho$ of $\zeta$, with residue $-m(\rho)/(\rho(\rho-1))$, where $m(\rho)$ is the multiplicity. Moreover, \hyperref[8JUH]{none of these poles is real}. So for $\zeta$ only case (a) in the proof of the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound} can occur.

\emph{Explicit formula.} The \hyperref[8JUK]{residue theorem} gives, for $x \ge 1$,
\[ G_{\mathbf 1}(x) = \ln 2\pi - \sum_\rho \frac{x^\rho}{\rho(\rho - 1)} - \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{x^{-2n}}{2n(2n+1)}, \]
where $\rho$ runs over the nontrivial zeros of $\zeta$, counted with multiplicity. Each zero $\rho = \beta + i\gamma$ contributes an oscillation $x^\rho = x^\beta e^{i\gamma\ln x}$ of size $x^\beta$.

\emph{Taylor coefficients.} Since $\int_1^\infty x^\rho\, x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac{1}{s - \rho}$ for $\Re s > \Re\rho$, the \hyperref[8JS2]{Mellin transform} of the explicit formula is
\begin{align*}
H_{\mathbf 1}(s) = \frac{\ln 2\pi}{s} &- \sum_\rho \frac{1}{\rho(\rho-1)(s - \rho)} \\
&- \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{1}{2n(2n+1)(s + 2n)}.
\end{align*}
Hence, for $\Re z > 1$ and $k \ge 0$, the coefficients $A_k(z) = \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}H_{\mathbf 1}^{(k)}(z)$ are
\begin{align*}
A_k(z) = \frac{\ln 2\pi}{z^{k+1}} &- \sum_\rho \frac{1}{\rho(\rho-1)(z - \rho)^{k+1}} \\
&- \sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{1}{2n(2n+1)(z + 2n)^{k+1}}.
\end{align*}
Each pole $p$ of $H_{\mathbf 1}$, with residue $c$, contributes $c/(z - p)^{k+1}$, so for large $k$ the poles nearest to $z$ dominate. The \hyperref[8JUP]{one-sided bound} uses the integral for $A_k$, and the \hyperref[8JUM]{lower bound} uses this series directly.

\emph{What the theorem says here.} For $\psi = \mathbf 1$ the \hyperref[8JK2]{interval estimate} only asks that $G_{\mathbf 1}(x) \le x^{1/2 + 9\varepsilon}$ on sparse intervals $[Y, Y^{3/2}]$. The \hyperref[8JUQ]{zeta interval criterion}, applied with $\theta=1/2+9\varepsilon$ for every sufficiently small $\varepsilon>0$, rules out nontrivial zeros to the right of the critical line. The functional equation then gives the Riemann hypothesis. Conversely, if the Riemann hypothesis holds, then $|x^\rho| = \sqrt x$ and $\rho(1 - \rho) = |\rho|^2$ for every zero, and $\sum_\rho \frac{1}{\rho(1-\rho)} = 2 + \gamma - \ln 4\pi \approx 0.0462$ \cite{Davenport}. With $\sum_{n \ge 1} \frac{1}{2n(2n+1)} = 1 - \ln 2 < 0.307$, the explicit formula then gives the much stronger two-sided bound
\[ |G_{\mathbf 1}(x) - \ln 2\pi| \le 0.0462\sqrt x + 0.307 \qquad (x \ge 1). \]

\emph{Graphs.} Panel (a) shows $G_{\mathbf 1}(x)$ for $1 \le x \le 10^7$, on a logarithmic $x$-axis, with this bound dashed.
\begin{center}
\textbf{(a)} $G_{\mathbf 1}(x)$ and the bound $\ln 2\pi \pm (0.0462\sqrt x + 0.307)$, for $1 \le x \le 10^7$
\end{center}
\includegraphics[width=1, alt=G_1(x) for x from 1 to 10 million on a logarithmic axis, oscillating with amplitude up to about 45, well inside the dashed curves ln 2 pi plus or minus (0.0462 sqrt x + 0.307)]{/static/figures/zeta-smoothed-sum.svg}
Panel (b) shows $(G_{\mathbf 1}(x) - \ln 2\pi)/\sqrt x$ for $10 \le x \le 10^7$, together with the explicit formula cut off after the first zero pair $\frac12 \pm 14.1347\,i$, which is a pure wave in $\ln x$, and after the first ten zero pairs, which already follows the data closely.
\begin{center}
\textbf{(b)} $\bigl(G_{\mathbf 1}(x) - \ln 2\pi\bigr)/\sqrt x$ and the explicit formula with the first one and ten zero pairs, for $10 \le x \le 10^7$
\end{center}
\includegraphics[width=1, alt=The normalized smoothed sum oscillating with size about 0.01 to 0.02, the first zero pair giving a pure wave and the first ten zero pairs following the data closely]{/static/figures/zeta-oscillation.svg}
Panel (c) shows $|A_k(\frac32 + i\tau)|$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$ and $0 \le \tau \le 50$, on a logarithmic scale; the dotted lines mark the heights $\gamma$ of the zeros $\frac12 + i\gamma$. Here $\sigma - \frac12 = 1$, so at $\tau = \gamma$ the zero $\rho = \frac12 + i\gamma$ contributes a term of size $1/|\rho|^2$ for every $k$, while the terms of the farther poles shrink as $k$ grows. For $k = 0$ and $1$ the pole at $s = 0$, with its comparatively large residue $\ln 2\pi \approx 1.84$, still dominates, and the zeros only make small dents. From $k = 2$ on, peaks of height about $1/|\rho|^2$ appear at the zeros. A zero $\beta + i\gamma$ with $\beta > \frac12$ would instead give peaks growing like $(\frac32 - \beta)^{-k}$, which is what the \hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound} detects.
\begin{center}
\textbf{(c)} $|A_k(\frac32 + i\tau)|$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$ and $0 \le \tau \le 50$; dotted lines at the zero heights $\gamma$
\end{center}
\includegraphics[width=1, alt=The absolute values of A_0 to A_3 on the line Re s = 3/2 for tau from 0 to 50 on a logarithmic scale, with peaks at the heights of the zeros of zeta appearing from k = 2 on]{/static/figures/zeta-taylor-coefficients.svg}', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 141;

UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{(i)} The right-hand sides in \ref{8JS6}(i) are meromorphic on $\C$. At $s = 1$ the singular parts cancel: for $\chi \ne \mathbf 1$ because $-L''(1,\chi)/L(1,\chi) = -b_\chi$, and for $\chi = \mathbf 1$ because $-\zeta''(s)/\zeta(s) = \frac{1}{s-1} - \gamma + O(s-1)$ gives
\[ -\frac{\zeta''(s)}{\zeta(s)}\,\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \frac{1}{(s-1)^2} - \frac{1+\gamma}{s-1} + O(1). \]
At a zero $\rho$ of $L(s,\chi)$ with $\Re\rho > 0$ (so $\rho \ne 0, 1$), $-L''(s,\chi)/L(s,\chi)$ has a simple pole with residue $-m_\chi(\rho)$, which gives the stated residue.

\emph{(ii)} By (i) and \ref{8JS6}(ii), $\mathcal H_\psi$ extends meromorphically to $\Re s > 0$, its poles are simple and lie in $Z$, and at $\rho \in Z$
\[ \operatorname{Res}_{s=\rho} \mathcal H_\psi = \frac12 \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi\, \frac{-m_\chi(\rho) - m_{\bar\chi}(\rho)}{\rho(\rho-1)} = -\frac{S_\rho}{\rho(\rho-1)}, \]
which is nonzero because \hyperref[8JS5]{$S_\rho > 0$}.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 101;

UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{(i)} Since
\[ \int_n^\infty \Bigl(\frac xn - 1\Bigr) x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac{n^{-s}}{s(s-1)} \]
and the series of the \hyperref[8JUS]{logarithmic derivative} converges absolutely, we may integrate term by term:
\begin{align*}
\int_1^\infty R_\chi(x)\, x^{-s-1}\,dx
  &= \frac{1}{s(s-1)} \sum_{n \ge 1} \Lambda(n)\chi(n)\, n^{-s} \\
  &= -\frac{L''(s,\chi)}{L(s,\chi)}\,\frac{1}{s(s-1)}.
\end{align*}
The remaining terms come from $\int_1^\infty x \cdot x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac1{s-1}$ and $\int_1^\infty x\ln x \cdot x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac1{(s-1)^2}$.

\emph{(ii)} Since \hyperref[8JS5]{$\overline{L(\bar s,\chi)} = L(s,\bar\chi)$}, we have $\overline{G_\chi} = G_{\bar\chi}$, so $\Re G_\chi = \frac12(G_\chi + G_{\bar\chi})$. Now use the linearity of $\mathcal M$.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 106;

UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{Idea.} Expand the local count in characters, use the large weights to read off the sum at scale $x$, and average in two stages.

\emph{(1)} \textbf{Inertia averaging gives the Fourier expansion at every prime.} For a character $\xi$ of $B$ set $c_\xi(p^i)=|I_p|^{-1}\sum_{v\in I_p}\xi(iF_p+v)$. For $g\in B$, orthogonality gives
$$
\frac1f\sum_{\xi\in\widehat B}\overline{\xi(g)}c_\xi(p^i)
=\frac2{|I_p|}\mathbf 1_{g-iF_p\in I_p}.
$$
Here $2=|B|/f$. Substituting this in \ref{8JU9} and using $(a/p^i-w)_+=w((a/w)/p^i-1)_+$ gives $wG_\xi(a/w)$ together with the main term $M(a,w)$. The trivial character contributes the logarithmic term. The sign character equals $-1$ off $\Phi$ and gives the term $a(L''(1,\xi_c)/L(1,\xi_c))$.

\emph{(2)} \textbf{Averaging over $A_0$ leaves a coefficient $\delta_2/2$.} The $h_2$ large weights are indexed by a subset of $A_0$. Replace them by $b$, discard the small weights, and later return to $x$ using $|a_t/b-x|\le 1$. The \hyperref[8JUE]{weight estimates} control these replacements. For a large-weight index $z\in A_0$, the Fourier factors depending on $u,z$ are $\xi(-u)\xi(-z)$, where $A$ is embedded in $B$. Their average over $u\in A_0$ vanishes unless $\xi|_{A_0}=1$. In that case the factor in $z$ is also $1$, giving $b h_2/f=b\delta_2/2$ at each level and translate.

\emph{(3)} \textbf{The coset distribution retains the powers of $\psi$ with their prescribed weights.} On $A/A_0$ the target distribution is $\lambda_\alpha=(3+\mathbf 1_{\alpha\in A_0})/(3o+1)$. Its Fourier coefficient at the trivial character is $1$. For any nontrivial character, the constant part sums to zero and the atom at the identity contributes $1/(3o+1)$. Thus the retained terms have exactly the coefficients of $\mathcal G_\psi$ in \ref{8JG8}. The error from the coset proportions and the return to $x$ is bounded in \cite[Sections 13--14]{Outline} by $4x^{1/2+\varepsilon}$ at the normalized scale. The sum $\Sigma(x)$ of \ref{8JUW} has at most $4o$ terms, and the joint choice of $q,d$ bounds each term by $x^{1/2+\varepsilon}$. Averaging the upper bound for $\Delta_u$ now gives the two inequalities.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 117;

UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{The unnormalized sum $R_{\mathbf 1}(x)$.} Apply the \hyperref[8JUJ]{integral for the positive part} with $y=x/n$, multiply by $\Lambda(n)$, and sum. Absolute convergence of $\sum_n \Lambda(n)n^{-s}=-\zeta''(s)/\zeta(s)$ on $\Re s=2$ allows the interchange, giving
\[
R_{\mathbf 1}(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_{2-i\infty}^{2+i\infty} I_x(s)\,ds,
\qquad I_x(s)=-\frac{\zeta''(s)}{\zeta(s)}\frac{x^s}{s(s-1)}.
\]

Shift the contour to the left. The residue theorem separates the four contributions to $R_{\mathbf 1}(x)$.
\begin{alignat*}{2}
s=1 &\quad x\ln x-(1+\gamma)x
&\qquad& \text{main term},\\
s=0 &\quad \ln 2\pi
&& \text{constant term},\\
s=\rho &\quad -\frac{m(\rho)x^\rho}{\rho(\rho-1)}
&& \text{nontrivial zero of order }m(\rho),\\
s=-2n &\quad -\frac{x^{-2n}}{2n(2n+1)}
&& \text{simple trivial zero }(n\ge 1).
\end{alignat*}

At $1$ the pole of $\zeta$ gives $-\zeta''(s)/\zeta(s)=1/(s-1)-\gamma+O(s-1)$. The factor $1/(s-1)$ in the kernel makes this a double pole. Using $x^s/s=x+x(\ln x-1)(s-1)+O_x((s-1)^2)$ gives the main term in the first row. At $0$ the kernel has residue $-1$, so the contribution is $\zeta''(0)/\zeta(0)=\ln 2\pi$. At a zero of order $m$, the residue of $-\zeta''/\zeta$ is $-m$.

For the contour shift, fix a positive odd integer $R$ and take the horizontal sides at heights $\pm T$ along a sequence with $\zeta''(s)/\zeta(s)=O_R((\ln T)^2)$ for $-R\le\Re s\le 2$ \cite{Davenport}. Their integrals tend to zero as $T\to\infty$ because the kernel is $O_{x,R}(T^{-2})$ there. On $\Re s=-R$, logarithmic differentiation of the functional equation gives $\zeta''(s)/\zeta(s)=O(\ln(R+|t|+2))$ uniformly in $R$ and $t=\operatorname{Im}s$. Indeed, the cotangent term is bounded on these lines, the gamma term is $O(\ln(R+|t|+2))$, and $\zeta''(1-s)/\zeta(1-s)$ is bounded by its Dirichlet series. The integral on the left line is therefore $O(x^{-R}\ln(R+2)/R)$, which tends to zero as $R\to\infty$ even at $x=1$. The zero count $N(T)=O(T\ln T)$ gives absolute convergence of the nontrivial-zero sum for each $x\ge 1$. The trivial-zero sum converges by comparison with $\sum n^{-2}$.

\emph{The normalized sum $G_{\mathbf 1}(x)$.} Subtract the residue at $1$ from $R_{\mathbf 1}(x)$, as in the \hyperref[8JF7]{definition of $G_{\mathbf 1}$}. The residue at $0$ gives the constant offset, a conjugate pair of nontrivial zeros gives an oscillation in $\ln x$, and the trivial zeros contribute terms that decay as $x\to\infty$.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 124;

UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = 'Take logarithmic derivatives of the Euler factors, using local uniform convergence of the product on $\Re s>1$, and expand by geometric series to obtain
\begin{align*}
-\frac{L''(s,\chi)}{L(s,\chi)}
  &= \sum_p \frac{\chi(p)\ln p\;p^{-s}}{1-\chi(p)p^{-s}} \\
  &= \sum_p\sum_{k\ge1}\chi(p^k)\ln p\;p^{-ks}
   = \sum_{n\ge1}\frac{\chi(n)\Lambda(n)}{n^s}.
\end{align*}
Absolute convergence follows from $\Lambda(n)\le\ln n$.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 128;

UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = 'By absolute convergence of the \hyperref[8JUS]{logarithmic derivative series}, we may integrate $\Psi_\chi(x)=\sum_n\chi(n)\Lambda(n)\mathbf 1_{n\le x}$ term by term. Using $\int_n^\infty x^{-s-1}\,dx=n^{-s}/s$ and \ref{8JUS} gives
\[ \int_1^\infty \Psi_\chi(x)\,x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac1s\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{\chi(n)\Lambda(n)}{n^s} = -\frac1s\,\frac{L''(s,\chi)}{L(s,\chi)}. \]', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-25 20:00:00', updated_by = 'codex' WHERE id = 129;

